 ## Overview
-The 3D Gaussian Splatting Differentiable Rasterizer renders a scene represented by a set of 3D Gaussian primitives into a 2D image by projecting them into screen space, sorting them by depth, and alpha-compositing their contributions [S3][S12, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently generating novel views and computing gradients for optimization by replacing volumetric ray-marching with a fast rasterization approach [S2][S7, unofficial]. Unlike NeRF-style volumetric rendering which integrates along rays, this method performs discrete alpha-blending of 2D elliptical kernels in image coordinates, enabling real-time rendering speeds and differentiable gradients with respect to Gaussian parameters (position, scale, rotation, opacity, color) and camera poses [S11, S12, unofficial].
+The 3D Gaussian Splatting Differentiable Rasterizer renders a scene represented by a set of 3D Gaussian primitives into a 2D image by projecting them to screen space, sorting them by depth, and accumulating their contributions via alpha compositing [S4][S11, unofficial]. Unlike volumetric ray marching used in NeRFs, this method operates in object order, rasterizing 2D elliptical kernels (splats) directly onto the image plane [S9][S15, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its specific use of a tile-based, front-to-back alpha blending pipeline that is fully differentiable with respect to all Gaussian parameters (position, scale, rotation, opacity) and camera poses, enabling end-to-end gradient-based optimization [S14, S15, unofficial].
 
