+### Isotropic Loss
+A regularisation term applied during RGB-D optimization to prevent Gaussians from over-stretching along the viewing axis, particularly in textureless regions [S17, unofficial]. Standard rasterisation does not account for elongation along the viewing axis, causing Gaussians to over-stretch without isotropic regularisation [S17, unofficial]. On TUM RGB-D, ATE RMSE changes from 1.43 cm (w/o $E_{iso}$) to 1.47 cm (Ours). On Replica RGB-D, Ours achieves 2.25 cm average ATE RMSE [S17, unofficial]. On TUM RGB-D, isotropic regularisation yields only marginal performance difference. On Replica RGB-D, it significantly improves camera tracking [S17, unofficial].
+
+### Normal Prediction from Gaussians
+Approximating the normal direction of a Gaussian using the axis with the minimum scaling coefficient, assuming Gaussians become flat, disc-like during optimization [S4]. Geometric normal for Gaussian $i$: $\hat{\boldsymbol{n}}_i = R \cdot \text{OneHot}(\arg\min(s_1, s_2, s_3))$, where $R \in SO(3)$ is the rotation matrix from quaternion $q$, and $s \in \mathbb{R}^3$ are scaling coefficients [S4]. Rotation matrix $R$ from quaternion $q=(w,x,y,z)^\top$: $R = \begin{bmatrix} 1 - 2(y^2+z^2) & 2(xy-wz) & 2(xz+wy) \\ 2(xy+wz) & 1 - 2(x^2+z^2) & 2(yz-wx) \\ 2(xz-wy) & 2(yz+wx) & 1 - 2(x^2+y^2) \end{bmatrix}$ [S4].
+
+### Parallel N-Dimensional Gaussian Rasterization
+A point-based alpha-blending technique for rasterizing feature maps alongside RGB, maintaining matching sizes for both to mitigate inconsistent spatial resolution inherent in tile-based rasterization. Dimensions are flexible to adapt to various foundational models [S18]. Rasterize features using ParallelRasterizer with inputs X, C, Σ, A, F, V to produce rendered feature map Fs [S18]. If refinement step, iterate over Gaussians (x, q, c, α, f): if opacity α < ε or Gaussian is too large, remove it [S18]. Learning rate for feature rendering optimization using Adam optimizer [S18]. Learning rate for the Speed-up Module feature decoder network using Adam optimizer [S18]. Image resolution strategy: render at 4 times lower resolution and upsample twice after 250 and 500 iterations [S18]. Standard exponential decay scheduling used similar to reference [11] [S18].
+
+### Simplified Gaussian Rasterization
+Rasterizes Gaussians to compute accumulated weights and max counts without full color/SH evaluation [S19, unofficial]. 1. Pack args (omits `flag_max_count`, includes `culling`). 2. Call `_C.rasterize_gaussians_simp(*args)`. 3. Return `color, radii, accum_weights_ptr, accum_weights_count, accum_max_count` [S19, unofficial].
+
+### Depth-aware Gaussian Rasterization
+Rasterizes Gaussians while computing depth, discriminants, and group indices for depth-based rendering [S19, unofficial]. 1. Pack args (omits `flag_max_count`, includes `culling`). 2. Call `_C.rasterize_gaussians_depth(*args)`. 3. Return dict with keys: render, out_pts, rendered_depth, discriminants, gidx, accum_alpha [S19, unofficial].
