+### Differentiable Bilinear Point Splatting
+A rasterization technique where each point is splatted to the closest 2x2 pixels after projection, avoiding aliasing and approximate gradients associated with one-pixel point rendering [S20]. Blending: Blend all $N$ points in depth order using the computed opacities and weights, following the standard alpha-compositing formulation (Eq. 1 in source, implied standard transmittance/opacity blend) [S20]. Projection: $p = P \cdot V \cdot p_w$, where $p_w = (x, y, z)^T$ is the point in world coordinates, and $P$ and $V$ are the intrinsic and extrinsic camera matrices respectively. Result is image coordinates $p = (u, v, d)^T$ [S20]. Opacity Weighting: $\alpha = \alpha_h \cdot (1 - |u - u_i|) \cdot (1 - |v - v_i|)$, where $(u_i, v_i)^T$ are the center points of the 2x2 closest pixels ($i \in \{0..3\}$). This weights the point's contribution based on its projected position [S20]. Differs from TRIPS [19] which uses trilinear interpolation into an image pyramid based on a learned per-point radius. This method renders high-quality feature images without interpolation with respect to a third dimension [S20].
+
+### 3DGS Optimization with Dual Loss
+End-to-end adjustment of Gaussian primitive attributes using differentiable rasterization and a combined loss function to match enhanced synthetic views [S21]. Project 3D Gaussians onto the 2D view plane [S21]. Perform alpha blending using an efficient tile-based rasterizer to synthesize final pixel colors [S21]. Backpropagate gradients through the entire differentiable rendering pipeline [S21]. Update all trainable parameters of the Gaussian primitives using the Adam optimizer [S21]. L = (1 - λ)L_L1 + λ*L_D-SSIM, where λ = 0.2 [S21]. λ: Weight for D-SSIM loss, set to 0.2 [S21].
