 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot T)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $T$ is the number of tiles, but effectively linear in practice due to culling and tiling [S11, unofficial]. The sorting step is $O(N \log N)$ per tile [S11, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N + H \cdot W)$ for storing Gaussian attributes and image buffers [S11, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Enables real-time rendering (30+ FPS) on modern GPUs for scenes with hundreds of thousands of Gaussians [S12, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High rendering speed, differentiable, supports view-dependent colors via SH [S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians; large scenes may require chunked training or LOD [S10].
-*   **When to Use:** Novel view synthesis, real-time rendering, differentiable optimization [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes requiring high-fidelity volumetric effects (e.g., fog, smoke) that are not well-approximated by Gaussians [S7, unofficial].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+The rasterization is $O(N)$ for projection and binning, and $O(H \cdot W \cdot K)$ for compositing, where $K$ is the average number of Gaussians per pixel. The tile-based approach ensures that only relevant Gaussians are processed per pixel, making it significantly faster than volumetric ray marching [S9]. Memory usage is dominated by the storage of Gaussian parameters and intermediate buffers for sorting and compositing [S13, unofficial].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+The method is designed for real-time performance on modern GPUs. The original 3DGS implementation achieves high frame rates (e.g., >30 FPS) for high-resolution images (e.g., 1024x1024) on consumer-grade GPUs [S15, unofficial].
+
+**Scaling:**
+Performance scales linearly with the number of Gaussians $N$ and image resolution $H \times W$. Optimizations such as kernel fusion and parallel N-dimensional rasterization can further improve throughput [S16, unofficial][S18].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** High rendering speed, differentiability for end-to-end training, high visual quality [S9][S15, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Memory consumption can be high for very large scenes with millions of Gaussians [S13, unofficial]. The method assumes linear projection; non-linear distortions require variants like 3DGUT [S1].
+
+**When to Use:**
+Use this method when real-time or near-real-time rendering of a learned 3D Gaussian scene is required, and gradients are needed for optimization [S15, unofficial].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+Avoid if the scene requires exact handling of non-linear camera distortions without approximation (use 3DGUT) or if memory constraints are extremely tight and $N$ is very large [S13, unofficial][S1].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S3]. It builds on the concept of splatting from computer graphics and adapts it for differentiable neural rendering [S2]. The method represents a scene as a set of 3D Gaussians, which are optimized from images using gradient descent [S11, unofficial]. The differentiable rasterizer is a key component that allows gradients to flow from the rendered image back to the Gaussian parameters [S12, unofficial]. The original implementation used CUDA kernels for high performance [S12, unofficial]. Subsequent work has explored various optimizations and variants, such as vertex shader implementations, unscented transforms, and normal estimation [S1, S3].
+The 3D Gaussian Splatting Differentiable Rasterizer was introduced in the paper "3D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S4][S19, unofficial]. It builds upon the concept of splatting from computer graphics and adapts it for differentiable rendering of 3D Gaussians. The method has become a standard technique for novel view synthesis and 3D scene reconstruction due to its speed and quality [S3, S9].
 
